There are so many platforms where one can stay sexy and not get murdered these days. HBO is the latest to get in business with Karen Kilgariff and Georgia Hardstark 's My Favorite Murder , turning their hit true crime comedy podcast into a docuseries. Also called My Favorite Murder , the four-part project chronicles the pair's 2025 sold-out American tour. It follows the format of their long-running podcast &#x2014; the women trade grim stories with a respectful dose of levity &#x2014;&#xA0;while also turning an eye to the devoted community of listers (aka "Murderinos") who have followed the pair for over a decade. "The question everyone asks us is always, 'Why do women love true crime so much?'" Kilgariff told The Hollywood Reporter earlier this year . "So we&#x2019;re letting them tell you. We can&#x2019;t ever answer that correctly. Other people have answers, but they&#x2019;re all personal, so let&#x2019;s get them." Related Stories TV Melissa Gorga to Debut 'Sunday Sauce' Video Podcast This December (Exclusive) Business How 'The Basement Yard' Host Joe Santagato Got Into the World of 'Hot Ones'

Premiering Dec. 3, the series airs on Thursdays through the rest of 2026 &#x2014; with episodes recorded during Chicago, Pasadena, Boston and Brooklyn shows. It is executive produced by Kilgariff and Hardstark, as well as showrunner, Page Hurwitz. The series comes from Liz Garbus and Dan Cogan's Story Syndicate as well as HBO Documentary Films . Turning 10 years old in January, this is not My Favorite Murder 's first pivot to TV. Kilgariff and Hardstark's Exactly Right Media signed a pact with Netflix last December to bring a video version of the podcast to the streamer where it now airs weekly. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

HBO released these summaries of its My Favorite Murder series, which will naturally stream on HBO Max as well. Episode 1: Chicago Debut date: Thursday, Dec. 3 (9:00-9:59 p.m. ET/PT) At The Chicago Theater, Karen and Georgia dig into the shocking hate crime murders of a prominent mid-western hairstylist and a noted cosmetic surgeon and then tell the fascinating tale of Alice Clement &#x2013; the first female detective in the Prohibition-era city. Episode 2: Pasadena Debut date: Thursday, Dec. 10 (9:00-9:58 p.m. ET/PT) At The Pasadena Civic Auditorium, in their hometown of the wider Los Angeles area, the podcasters look into the case of the &#x201C;80s sex, drugs, and &#x2018;Just Sweats&#x2019; insurance scandal,&#x201D; as well as the bizarre, botched kidnapping of Frank Sinatra Jr. Episode 3: Boston Debut date: Thursday, Dec. 17 (9:00-9:59 p.m. ET/PT) At The Boch Center Wang Theatre, Karen and Georgia dissect the still unsolved disappearance of wife and mother Joan Risch in 1961, which some see as the inspiration for the film Gone Girl, as well as the famed 1978 blizzard that ground the city to a halt and led to some unexpected &#x201C;sleepovers.&#x201D; Episode 4: Brooklyn Debut date: Thursday, Dec. 24 (9:00-10:00 p.m. ET/PT) At the Kings Theatre, the hosts take a deeper look at the infamous Scarsdale diet doctor murder, and then follow the peculiar case of the red bees of Red Hook, Brooklyn that led to a shocking discovery in the basement of a nearby maraschino cherry warehouse.

EXCLUSIVE: Ryusuke Hamaguchi&#x2019;s All of a Sudden has won the 2026 Indie Film Site Network Advocate Award , marking the first time a narrative feature has won the accolade.

The finalists for the 2026 IFSN Advocate Award were Poh Si Teng&#x2019;s American Doctor , Walter Thompson-Hern&#xE1;ndez&#x2019;s If I Go Will They Miss Me , Yuval Abraham and Rachel Szor&#x2019;s NAZA , Ross McElwee&#x2019;s Remake , and Barbara Kopple&#x2019;s Union Town .

The IFSN award, created in 2022 to &#x201C;highlight independent films each year that illuminate a humanitarian issue with a singular artistic vision,&#x201D; comes with a singular prize for the winner: one million media impressions across the Indie Film Site Network, which represents The Film Stage, Hammer to Nail, IONCINEMA.com, Next Best Picture, RogerEbert.com, and Slant Magazine. Letterboxd is also contributing to the award. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News Ryūsuke Hamaguchi's 'All Of A Sudden' To Open AFI Silver's 2026 European Union Film Showcase -- Full Lineup News Gotham Awards Film Nominations Led By 'The Invite', 'Tender Loving Care', 'All Of A Sudden' & More

Previous IFSN Advocate Award winners include Julia Loktev&#x2019;s My Undesirable Friends: Part I - Last Air in Moscow ; Basel Adra, Hamdan Ballal, Yuval Abraham, and Rachel Szor&#x2019;s No Other Land ; D. Smith&#x2019;s Kokomo City ; and Shaunak Sen&#x2019;s All That Breathes . Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

&#x201C;Bringing a staggering level of humanity and insight to life&#x2019;s mysteries throughout his career, Ryusuke Hamaguchi has delivered his most universally empathetic film yet with All of a Sudden ,&#x201D; Jordan Raup, co-founder of IFSN and editor-in-chief and co-founder of The Film Stage, said in a statement.

&#x201C;Led by luminous performances from Virginie Efira and Tao Okamoto, this drama embodies everything the ideal IFSN Advocate Award encompasses: living everyday life with humble compassion toward anyone you encounter. That Hamaguchi also eloquently dissects how modern-era capitalism is rotting out the globe is nothing short of a miracle.&#x201D;

All of a Sudden premiered at the 2026 Cannes Film Festival, where Virginie Efira and Tao Okamoto shared the Best Actress prize, and subsequently played at the Locarno Film Festival (where Virginie Efira received the honorary Leopard Club award), the Toronto International Film Festival, New York Film Festival, BFI London Film Festival, Chicago International Film Festival, where the film won the Silver Hugo for Best Screenplay, and the Montclair Film Festival, where the film won the Audience Award for World Cinema. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The film is Japan's entry for the 2027 Academy Awards and was nominated for four Gotham Awards, including Best International Feature, and a British Independent Film Award for Best International Independent Film.

All of a Sudden opens in theaters on November 25 via Neon.

John Muszynski never worked for the company known at various times as Time Warner, AOL Time Warner, WarnerMedia or Warner Bros. Discovery. He did, however, have a hand in at least one part of its operations.

About 25 years ago, when &#x201C;cable&#x201D; represented a rapidly growing line of business and &#x201C;streaming&#x201D; had something to do with water, Muszynski was on a hunting trip outside of Atlanta with Mark Lazarus, at the time a senior executive at Warner&#x2019;s TV operations. Lazarus says the pair were hunting for quail. Muszynski, one of the industry&#x2019;s most senior — and most seasoned — media buyers , says the duo was chasing pheasant. No matter what the game was, the two executives devised what would eventually become a much greater prize. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Is ‘I Play Rocky’ Original or Adapted? Stallone’s ‘Rocky’ Origin Story Faces a ‘Barbie’-Style Oscar Dilemma (EXCLUSIVE) Former BBC Studios, NBCUniversal Exec Jenny Groom Named Head of MGM Alternative

&#x201C;What I remember asking is, &#x2018;What can we do as a company that would make you want to spend more money with us?&#x2019;&#x201D; recalls Lazarus, now the CEO of Versant, the owner of MS NOW, CNBC and other media properties. &#x201C;He said, &#x2018;I need something to compete with MTV. Right now, they are the only place that has the young people.&#x2019; We had Adult Swim at the time, but it was only on three hours a week. &#x2018;It&#x2019;s not enough to move money, not enough leverage for me with the Viacom folks,&#x2019;&#x201D; Muszynski told him. &#x201C;I went back to the group that did content, and said, &#x2018;We have to try to expand Adult Swim,&#x2019;&#x201D; Lazarus remembers.

Over time, the gambit proved fruitful. Adult Swim, once a small piece of the schedule on Warner&#x2019;s Cartoon Network, became a regular nighttime offering and captured the attention of a very difficult audience to reach — young men. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;He was absolutely a catalyst, and opened our eyes,&#x201D; says Lazarus. &#x201C;He gave us a reason to really take a bigger risk.&#x201D;

Someone else will have to offer such suggestions in the future. Muszysnki, the U.S. chairman of Publicis Media Exchange, is stepping away from the role. And while he will serve as a consultant to the company in 2027, his departure will mark the end of an era, of sorts, as media becomes much more reliant on technology that seems to be moving many of its best-known outlets closer to obsolescence.

Over nearly half a century, Muszynski worked for French ad giant Publicis Groupe, and before that the legendary U.S. advertising company Leo Burnett. His job? Placing billions of ad dollars across the U.S. media sector on behalf of clients that have encompassed blue-chip spenders ranging from the U.S. Army to McDonald&#x2019;s, Kellogg&#x2019;s and Molson Coors. He never ran a movie studio, a TV-news division or a sports operation. He never put together a must-see primetime TV schedule or devised a streaming-video hit. If anyone ever gave out an award for &#x201C;Most Influential Media Executive the Average Person Has Never Heard Of,&#x201D; Muszysnki would be a candidate to win.

"As the industry evolved, John not only evolved with it, but helped lead in ways others couldn't," says Jeff Collins, president of advertising sales, marketing and brand partnerships for Fox Corp.

Behind the scenes, his efforts held massive sway. The ad dollars he vetted helped the independent female-focused Oxygen cable network launch in 2000; boosted the presence of college football on ESPN; and lent support to new techniques to measure TV audiences that rely on whether viewers engage with what they watch, rather than how many people passively stare at whatever fills their screens. &#x201C;John was always someone who understood the power of picking up the phone and saying, &#x2018;I&#x2019;ve got an idea for you. Would you be interested in hearing it?&#x201D; says Ed Erhardt, who previously ran advertising sales for the entire ESPN portfolio at Disney. &#x201C;Of course, we would always be interested in that.&#x201D;

And Muszynski did it all while Publicis grew, winning accounts from Anheuser-Busch InBev, Microsoft, Kenvue, Mars, Walmart, and, more recently, Pepsi. Publicis Media, for which Muszynski works, managed $62.4 billion worth of media spent around the world in 2025, according to COMvergence, a tracker of such activity. The figure marks a rise of 12.8%, or $7 billion, over what the company managed in 2024. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Muszynski wasn&#x2019;t the one charged with winning new accounts. Still, Publicis&#x2019; rise against competitors like WPP and Omnicom was fueled in part by Muszynski&#x2019;s expertise, says Erhardt. &#x201C;John Muszynski had a huge impact on that, his relationships with the sellers, but also the clients.&#x201D;

Whether the media companies liked all of Muszynski&#x2019;s ideas or not, they had to listen to them. Those who didn&#x2019;t agree might find some of their ad dollars going to a competitor.

At a gathering of top TV sales executives in 2018, Muszynski was blunt. He cautioned the assemblage they might have to &#x201C;deliver last year&#x2019;s plan at the same budget, maybe less,&#x201D; and warned them that the best way to win new ad dollars was to help advertisers bolster their sales. &#x201C;All of you who are looking for increased budgets, the best way to do that is to help solve our clients&#x2019; problems,&#x201D; he said at the time.

Indeed, many companies have tried to do critical deals with Muszynski first. Because Publicis works on behalf of so many top advertisers, his take on the market is usually accurate. He often has a better sense than others of whether ad budgets are fat or lean. &#x201C;We always arranged John&#x2019;s deal first,&#x201D; says Joe Abruzzese, who logged stints as the head of sales for CBS and Discovery Communications. &#x201C;We really built our business on the way John was able to maneuver his clients.&#x201D;

In the yearly TV ad-sales market known as the &#x201C;upfront, &#x201D; ad commitments don&#x2019;t always end up in the bank. Advertisers may end up pulling the dollars they promised or asking for delays of their commercial orders. Not with Muszynski, say multiple sales executives.

"John doesn&#x2019;t over buy and then try to make it up,&#x201D; says Charlie Collier, the media industry veteran who has worked in senior roles at Roku, Fox and AMC. &#x201C;He has always been the guy who calls it like he sees it: &#x2018;I&#x2019;m not going promise all sorts of things that I then have to chase later. I&#x2019;m going to tell you like I see it and make deals I know I can live up to.&#x2019; And he always did.&#x201D;

That trust in the way Muszynski conducted himself led to unorthodox negotiations. Abruzzese says he once convinced Muszynski to add $200 million to an &#x201C; upfront &#x201D; plan while sitting with him on the beach — and still has the piece of suntan-lotion-stained paper that displays the agreement. In another year, says Abruzzese, he and Muszynski worked out details on a tablecloth at a fancy dinner — and Muszynski had to pay the venue $50 so he could bring the linen back to his office where his colleagues could see the details. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Such transactions have been going on for years. While working at Oxygen Media ahead of the launch of the network in 2000, says Collier, &#x201C;We negotiated with him and he was terrific. We got a deal done. A few months later, he called: &#x2018;You really screwed me!&#x2019; He had just picked up a client from another agency, and he thought the rates they had gotten were better. I said &#x2018;They&#x2019;re not. I remember this deal, and you came on with us in pre-launch, and we didn&#x2019;t give someone more favorable terms.&#x201D; To reassure Muszynski, says Collier, &#x201C;I literally printed out our entire rate card, packaged it with women&#x2019;s razors and products that were aimed at women, and put in a pink and blue box. I wrote, &#x2018;You were good enough to come and support us in the early stages and here is our entire rate card.&#x2019; That&#x2019;s the kind of trust I share with him. I don&#x2019;t think I&#x2019;ve sent a rate card to a client before or since.&#x201D;

Those sorts of exchanges are becoming less frequent. More advertisers use reams of algorithms and data brews to inform the way they spread their commercials across streaming outlets and FAST channels. Media-buying agencies and even legacy media companies have begun to tinker with the idea of letting AI find the right inventory and the best schedules on which commercials should run. Some marketing executives seem more interested in whether they can spur clicks to an Amazon cart or interactions with a QR code than they do in crafting a memorable piece of promotion that works because it was aligned with a particular program or media property.

In an era that relies less on deals sealed with handshakes, Muszynski still had his clients&#x2019; confidence. But his successors may have a harder time winning it. The practice of figuring out how to place commercials and get a good price for them won&#x2019;t collapse because of John Muszynski&#x2019;s exit. Still, it might be left standing on shakier ground.

